==Portuguese==
{{wp|lang=pt}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|pt|es|gringo}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA}}
* {{rhyme|pt|ĩɡu|s=2}}
* {{hyph|pt|gri|go}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|f=+}}

# {{lb|pt|Brazil|colloquial}} a [[foreigner]], especially one from a Northern country and especially one from the United States
#: {{syn|pt|estrangeiro}}
# {{lb|pt|Rio Grande do Sul|colloquial}} someone of (more commonly) [[Italian]] or (more rarely) [[European]] descent.
#: {{syn|pt|colono}}

===Adjective===
{{pt-adj}}

# {{lb|pt|Brazil|colloquial}} [[foreign]] {{gloss|from another country, especially the United States or another developed one}}
#: {{ux|pt|Aquele cara ali é '''gringo'''.|t=That dude over there is a '''foreigner'''.}}
#: {{ux|pt|Comprei um telefone '''gringo'''.|I bought a '''foreign-made''' telephone.}}
# {{lb|pt|Rio Grande do Sul|colloquial}} someone of (more commonly) [[Italian]] or (more rarely) [[European]] descent.
#: {{syn|pt|colono}}

===Usage notes===
* Unlike English and Spanish ''gringo'', this Portuguese term is not inherently offensive.
* The usage of ''gringo'' varies greatly between speakers and can have a broader meaning if compared to English and Spanish <ref>{{cite-journal|pt|author=Thaddeus Blanchette|title=Estrangeiro - Gringo - Brasileiro: Aproximação e afastamento entre brasileiros e não-brasileiros|journal=TRAVESSIA - Revista Do Migrante|number=44|location=São Paulo|publisher=Peres|year=2002|month=Sep.–Dec.|accessdate=2024-01-18|pages=18–23|doi=10.48213/travessia.i44.853}}</ref>:
** It can refer to any foreigner — uncommon usage and rarely applies to people of African or Latin American origin. 
** It refers only to Americans — also uncommon.
** It refers to people from the [[Global North]] exclusively, especially from Northern Europe, the US and Canada — the most common use of the term.

