==Spanish==

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{m|es|griego||Greek}}, particularly from the phrase {{m|es||[[hablar]] [[en]] [[griego]]|to speak Greek}}, with a similar connotation to the English phrase {{m|en|it's all Greek to me}}. Possibly influenced by {{m|es|peregrino||pilgrim}}. Or else due to the ubiquity of the song {{w|Green Grow the Lilacs}} among the men who settled the interior of the American continent.

===Pronunciation===
{{es-pr}}

===Noun===
{{es-noun|m|f=+}}

# {{lb|es|sometimes|pejorative|Latin America}} a fair-skinned [[foreigner]] whose native language is not Spanish
#: {{syn|es|gabacho<q:Spain, Mexico>|guiri<q:Spain>}}
#* {{quote-book|es|year=1786|title=Diccionario castellano con las voces de Ciencias y Artes y sus correspondientes en las 3 lenguas francesa, latina e italiana|author=Esteban de Terroros y Pando; Beatriz Varela|newversion=quoted in|editor2=Félix Rodríguez González|title2=Spanish Loanwords in the English Language: A Tendency towards Hegemony Reversal|chapter2=Ethnic nicknames of Spanish origin in American English|location2=Berlin; New York|year2=1996|publisher2=Mouton de Gruyter|url2=http://books.google.com/books?id=09NEuGHh2R8C&pg=PA143&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q&f=false|isbn=9783110148459|page2=143|passage=... '''''gringos''''', llaman en Málaga a los extranjeros, que tienen cierta especie de acento, que los priva de una locución fácil, y natural Castellana; y en Madrid dan el mismo, y por la misma causa con particularidad a los irlandeses.
|translation=gringos is what, in Malaga, they call foreigners who have a certain kind of accent that prevents them from speaking Castilian easily and naturally; and in Madrid they give the same name, in particular, to the Irish.
}}
# {{lb|es|sometimes|pejorative|Latin America}} an American (a person from the United States), especially a white American.
#: {{cot|es|angloamericano|estadounidense|norteamericano}}
#* {{quote-web
|es
|date=2008-10-08
|year=
|author=Antonio Caballero
|title=El negro gringo (o el gringo negro)
|site=Semana
|url=http://m.semana.com/opinion/articulo/el-negro-gringo-o-gringo-negro/96350-3
|accessdate=2014-08-01
|passage=Pero la realidad es más terca que la corrección política, y el hecho real es que Barack Obama, próximo presidente de los Estados Unidos, es un '''gringo''', y es un negro. O, si se prefiere así, es un negro, y es un '''gringo'''.
|translation=But the reality is more stubborn than political correctness, and the fact is that Barack Obama, the next president of the United States, is a gringo, and is a black. Or, if you so prefer, is a black, and a gringo.
}}

